k  like it's stopping the queen coming down  here to g7  which it is but it's not really setting  a trap so knight to  e7 makes it look like okay yes you  almost have to move the knight or the  pawn because if not then the queen could  just come down here on g7 and then  easily take the rook so it almost looks  like the knight's just moving out of the  way so that the rook is not going to be  taken very easily and potentially just  doesn't want to play g6 so  white many times we'll just go ahead and  take that pawn here on g7 black's going  to play rook to  g8 you know the rook is under attack so  pretty much has to move but now white  can just easily take this pawn here on  h7 and this is the trap because  if white takes these two pawns yes black  cannot castle on the king side anymore  that's completely okay  black is going to have a very good game  here because they have a lot of threats  and the first threat is the pawn taking  here on d4 this knight here on c3 is  being pinned  to the king by the bishop here on b4  so that is the first threat here that  white can just  lose the knight here on c3 with the pawn  taking here so how does white really  respond to this well if you look at  something like pawn to a3  this attacks the bishop well okay this  gets tricky because the move queen to a5  and this just says yeah if you want to  take my bishop that's fine  because now we can just come down here  and take with our queen here on  a1  and now we're also threatening to move  queen takes here on c1 they can't move  their bishop because it's being pinned  down by the queen so really they move  they need to move them knight to e2 for  two reasons one it's defending this  bishop here on c1 but also it's being  attacked by this pawn here on  d4 so if you look at this setup  all of a sudden white lost the rook here  on a1 not exactly what they want to  their knight on e2 is kind of stuck  because it's defending the bishop the  bishop can't move it's pinned down  because this knight's here this light  square bishop on f1 is not going  anywhere  the knight on g1 definitely can get out  so that's a move that white has here  and black's pretty well defended the  queen over here on h7 is kind of hanging  out by themselves and so black can  really do exactly what they want they  can get their other pieces involved into  the game  knight to b6 if they want or c6 if they  want to that's going to attack both the  pawn here on e5 as well as the pawn here  on b4 they can get their bishop involved  into the games they can start to push up  the other pawns here on the b file and  the a file so black has a lot of threats  here and it's going to be very difficult  for black to continue if we come back  maybe they decide okay probably not the  best idea to just take right there  so let's get the  the rook out of harm's way let's go  ahead and play  rook to b1 okay well in this case we go  ahead and take the knight here on c3 as  we talked about  they take our bishop  but then we can play queen to a2  now this is still threatening the rook  and the rook is going to fall any time  they take and they leave open this a  file we're just going to come down and  eventually take their rook here  so now they could play rook to e2  we're going to take their rook here on  b1  pawn takes here on c3 that's completely  fine  and then we can play something like  knight to d7 different ways of how you  want to get the knight involved into the  game but  this threatens this  pawn here on uh e5 it also allows if we  wanted to to get the knight to come back  here to f8 and have some protection in  there as well so a couple options uh  from white there but you can see black  even has more material here because  we're able not only to take that knight  but we're also able to take that rook  okay so let's come back we've already  looked at the fact that if they  don't play pawn to a3  then we're pretty much just going to  take with our pawn here on c3 they're  going to lose the knights  that's going to be pretty difficult  and so a3 makes some sense to attack the  bishop here but then we have the threat  of queen to a5 and they can't really  take with their pawn here  because then we're just going to take  their rook so what if they decide to  just leave that alone and start to  develop material on the king side of the  board recognizing they really have to  get material out start  to attack  black at some capacity or else they're  just going to get suffocated and lose so  maybe knight to f3  black's always going to take that knight  here on c3 with the pawn  pawn to b3 i think is the best move from  white if you look at the threats that  black has after the pawn takes here it's  a discovered attack that bishop is now  going to check the king and then white's  going to lose either the bishop or the  rook so i really think that white almost  has to play b3 if not they're definitely  going to lose material here  then black plays knight to d7  because  white does have the threat now of bishop  here to  d3  preparing to come over and attack here  on the king side of the board also they  need to be opening up to castle on the  king side to get some sort of safety  here  but now we can just come down take the  pawn here on g2 that's one thing to keep  in mind as soon as they play bishop here  to d3  we're just eyeing down and we can just  take this material here on g2  they could play knight to g5 threatening  something like queen takes here on f7  that would be  very bad for black  we can just go ahead and take the rook  yes we gave up a  rook  for a knight and a pawn but that's going  to be completely okay as far as the  situation that we have to worry about  here so after the bishop takes we can  now play knight to c6 this is attacking  this pawn right here we still have a lot  of threats they still can't take with  their pawn right here because we could  just take their rook up here  and they're going to have a difficult  time now you do have to be very cautious  from black standpoint because you have a  weakened king  there's not a lot of protection here the  knight here on d7 it's going to do a  good job of coming down here to f8 if it  needs to protect from the queen at some  point  but this is going to be very dynamic  from both sides and you can see that at  any given move  either side can fall apart but i do  think black has a good position in this  setup so that is  the double poison pawn trap so if we  come back it's it's really not the best  move for white to go ahead and take  this pawn here on g7 and if they do take  where it comes over here to g8  i think it gets even trickier if they  decide to take this pawn here on h7 they  could just come back to h6 the main  reason that queen two h6 is better is  let's just continue down the same lines  we're looking at pawn takes here on d4  this knight's being pinned down so play  a3 as we talked about queen two a5 but  now they can take and after we take the  rook here on a1  now you can see that this queen is  protecting the bishop now before they  pretty much had to play knight to e2  because that was not only  the knights being under attack so it has  to move  and if they move anywhere else the  bishop's not defended so the queen could  just come here to see one it's gonna  fall there's nowhere else because it's  being pinned  but because the queen is actually  protecting it's on these dark squares  then like it's free to actually go  somewhere else so then i can come here  to b5 it's attacking the pawn here on d4  it's also no longer blocking off this  light square bishop so it's going to be  much better for white if they don't  actually fall for the double poison pawn  trap  just go ahead and take that one poison  pawn i still think they're going to be  in a tough spot you can see the queen's  not going anywhere it's very easy for  black to get their own knight involved  into the game put a lot of pressure on  the opponent you know even just pushing  forward with both the pawns yes you do  lose your central pawn here  but as soon as the slice square bishop  moves black and come up take this  material here on g2 so a lot of stuff  that white has to deal with but i think  it's a much better position  if they play queen here to h6 than  taking the extra pawn here on h7 so that  is the double poison pawn trap hopefully  you guys enjoyed this video hope you  learned something hopefully if you like  playing the french you can lay this trap  for someone and they can fall on it  if you are playing as white and you run  up against this hopefully now you know  how to avoid  the worst lines possible so thank you  guys so much for watching the video i'll  see you guys in the next one

